+//! The instrument `lakelet/4.24-runbook-drill` is judged by: it reads `docs/runbook.md`, parses it
+//! into stages, runs the command each stage names **against a live store**, and reports what each
+//! stage owed.
+//!
+//! This file is **course content**, like `tests/budget.rs` and `tests/soak.rs`: the step ships it,
+//! `kiln run` keeps it, and `kiln reset lakelet/4.24-runbook-drill` puts it back. Editing it edits
+//! the measurement rather than the thing it measures — the page is the deliverable.
+//!
+//! # The shape a page must have
+//!
+//! This is the whole contract between `docs/runbook.md` and this instrument, and it is a decision
+//! rather than a convention: a procedure an instrument can walk has to be *data*.
+//!
+//! * **a stage** is a `###` heading carrying a number and a title (`### 3. Put the copy back`), and
+//!   the stages run from `1.` upwards with no number repeated and none skipped. A `####` heading is
+//!   prose inside the stage it stands in, and a heading above `###` ends the last stage;
+//! * **a stage owes five parts** ([`PARTS`]), on the page, every one of them:
+//!   * the **command** — a fenced `sh` block inside the stage holding **one** command. A line
+//!     starting with `#` is a comment, a blank line separates nothing (the command is one), and a
+//!     command wrapped onto the next line is read as one command;
+//!   * the **signal** — the counter or the number an operator watches while this stage runs. Every
+//!     name it puts in backticks has to be something **this project really reports**: a field a
+//!     daemon logs, a counter a harness prints, a number a page states. A signal nobody reports is
+//!     a reader sent to a dashboard that does not exist;
+//!   * the **state** — the name of the check this instrument makes after the command ran
+//!     ([`CHECKS`]). "It looked fine" is not an assertion, and a stage whose state nobody can check
+//!     is a stage nobody finishes;
+//!   * the **rollback** — what puts the deployment back: a command of this deployment, `none` for
+//!     an act that cannot be undone, or `nothing was taken` for a stage that changes nothing.
+//!     Which of the three it is *is judged*: an act that writes the store cannot say nothing was
+//!     taken, and an act that changes nothing should not invent a way back;
+//!   * the **audience** — who is told, by role;
+//! * **a stage is written for an operator, not for a shell.** The command is one of the programs
+//!   this deployment ships ([`PROGRAMS`]) and its arguments, and this instrument runs the program
+//!   directly rather than handing the line to a shell: a stage cannot smuggle in a pipeline, and a
+//!   command nobody has is a finding rather than a process that does something else. The
+//!   deployment's own variables ([`VARS`]) are the paths of the world this instrument built;
+//! * the page's **first numeral is the number of stages**, and this instrument holds the page to
+//!   it: a procedure that says it has eight stages and writes nine is two documents.
+//!
+//! # What it runs, and what it checks
+//!
+//! [`drill`] builds **a store of its own** through the real write path — payloads through `Spool`,
+//! a segment per batch through `Segments::seal` — starts `lakeletd` on it where a stage needs a
+//! live deployment, and walks the page's stages **in the page's order**: it runs each stage's
+//! command as the program it names and then makes that stage's check. The world is the deployment
+//! the page is written for: one store of two sealed segments, the eight series this project's own
+//! load generator spreads over, and a key ring with two keys on it.
+//!
+//! Nothing here reads a clock, and nothing here prints a path: the report is counts, stage numbers,
+//! the commands **as the page writes them** and verdicts, so the block `docs/runbook.md` quotes is
+//! the same bytes on every machine — and `lakelet/4.19-backup-restore`'s rule for its own page
+//! holds here: the numbers a page carries are re-run rather than trusted.
+//!
+//! # A gap is a finding, never a panic
+//!
+//! Every obligation this file finds is a [`Gap`] carrying the stage, the part and one sentence:
+//! a part a stage does not name, a command this deployment does not ship, a signal nobody reports,
+//! a state that is not a check, a rollback that is not a command, a command that exits non-zero.
+//! The reader is callable **on text** ([`Runbook::of`] takes a page from anywhere) because the spec
+//! that judges the reader has to hand it pages this file wrote.
+//!
+//! # Two ways to use it
+//!
+//! * `cargo test -p lakelet --test runbook_harness -- --nocapture` runs [`the_runbook_page_holds`]
+//!   and prints the report;
+//! * the same functions are what `course/tracks/lakelet/spec/4.24/runbook.rs` calls, through
+//!   `#[path = "runbook_harness.rs"] mod harness;` — the spec reaches the instrument instead of
+//!   writing a second one, which is what keeps one answer to "is this page runnable".
+
+#![allow(dead_code)] // a spec uses the part of the instrument it needs, not all of it
+
